# Scheduled jobs. These were WRITTEN, TESTED AND NEVER SCHEDULED — no cron entry, no systemd timer,
# and no worker container had ever existed on this host. Discovered 2026-07-31.
#
# Every one is a ONE-SHOT: the container runs, does its job, exits, and is removed. The compose
# service carries profiles:[jobs] so `up -d` can never start it as a service.
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin
CRON_TZ=UTC

# Inara rank + profile sync. ONCE A DAY, BATCHED — the adapter sends 30 commanders per request, and
# Inara ask for infrequent bundled calls rather than one per commander. 05:12 is off-peak against
# measured activity (peaks 01:00 and 20:00-21:00) and clear of the 04:17 janitor.
12 5 * * * root cd /srv/grims/repo && docker compose -f infra/docker/compose.prod.yml --env-file /srv/grims/.env --profile jobs run --rm worker node apps/worker/dist/inara-sync.js >> /var/log/grims-worker.log 2>&1

# Nickname + commander reconciliation. After the Inara sync, so it acts on fresh names.
40 5 * * * root cd /srv/grims/repo && docker compose -f infra/docker/compose.prod.yml --env-file /srv/grims/.env --profile jobs run --rm worker node apps/worker/dist/daily-audit.js >> /var/log/grims-worker.log 2>&1

# ── Embedding ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The ONLY jobs here that use the GPU. Everything else in the pipeline is a database write, which is
# why the card is idle during a galaxy import.
#
# EVERY source is embedded now. The earlier design embedded prose only, because the galaxy was
# believed to take "roughly three weeks" — measured on the actual card it is 104 rows/second at
# concurrency 8, so just over an hour, once, then only what changed. Embedding is ADDED, never
# substituted: "which stations in Deciat have a large pad" is still an exact lookup, because a
# similarity search answers that with Deciak.
#
# Cadences set by the squadron owner, 2026-08-01, and they follow how fast each source produces text
# somebody might immediately ask about — not how expensive it is. A sweep with nothing to do is one
# indexed query returning no rows.

# Forum answers — every 5 minutes. Somebody answers a question and walks away; the next member to
# ask should get that answer rather than being told nobody has covered it.
*/5 * * * * root cd /srv/grims/repo && docker compose -f infra/docker/compose.prod.yml --env-file /srv/grims/.env --profile jobs run --rm worker node apps/worker/dist/embed.js forum >> /var/log/grims-worker.log 2>&1

# Squadron flight logs — every 3 minutes. "Has anyone been to X" while a wing is actually there
# should answer yes.
*/3 * * * * root cd /srv/grims/repo && docker compose -f infra/docker/compose.prod.yml --env-file /srv/grims/.env --profile jobs run --rm worker node apps/worker/dist/embed.js journal >> /var/log/grims-worker.log 2>&1

# The roster — daily, at 06:05, just after its own ingest. It changes when somebody joins or is
# promoted, and neither happens by the minute.
5 6 * * * root cd /srv/grims/repo && docker compose -f infra/docker/compose.prod.yml --env-file /srv/grims/.env --profile jobs run --rm worker node apps/worker/dist/embed.js inara >> /var/log/grims-worker.log 2>&1

# Everything else — 06:20, after the 03:20 galaxy import and the 06:05 roster sweep. This is the one
# that carries the galaxy and Coriolis, and the only one that is ever long. Resumable by
# construction: the work remaining IS "rows with no vector", so a run that dies leaves the rest for
# tomorrow and re-running is never redundant.
20 6 * * * root cd /srv/grims/repo && docker compose -f infra/docker/compose.prod.yml --env-file /srv/grims/.env --profile jobs run --rm worker node apps/worker/dist/embed.js >> /var/log/grims-worker.log 2>&1

# ── Monthly promotions ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ★ THIS WAS NEVER SCHEDULED — found 2026-08-01 ★
#
# The engine was written, tested, and had a `promote.js` in the deployed image. Nothing ever ran it.
# Asked "did the auto promotions work on production", the answer was no, and the reason was that
# there was no cron entry for it anywhere — the word "promote" appeared in this file exactly once,
# inside a comment about something else.
#
# ★ WHY THE 1st, AND WHY 07:00 ★
#
# Qualification is a statement about a COMPLETED calendar month, so the earliest it can be answered
# for July is the 1st of August. 07:00 UTC puts it after the nightly ingests and the role sync, so
# it acts on a reconciled roster rather than on yesterday's.
#
# ★ --live AND --post ★
#
# Without --live it is a dry run that writes nothing, which is the safe default everywhere else and
# would be a silent no-op here. --post sends the result to the admin channel, because a promotion
# nobody is told about is a promotion nobody acts on.
#
# The squadron owner's instruction was "DO NOT START ASSIGNING PROMOTIONS UNTIL AUGUST 1ST 2026".
# That date has passed; this is the automation it was gating.
0 7 1 * * root cd /srv/grims/repo && docker compose -f infra/docker/compose.prod.yml --env-file /srv/grims/.env --profile jobs run --rm worker node apps/worker/dist/promote.js --live --post >> /var/log/grims-worker.log 2>&1

# Nightly reputation: experience for days flown, and badges for the totals that adds to.
#
# Votes and accepted answers award experience at the moment they happen — they are events. These
# two are not: "played today" is a fact about a DAY, and a badge is a threshold crossed by a total
# several different actions can move. Both are questions about accumulated state, and asking them
# once a night is cheaper and more correct than detecting the crossing inside every write.
#
# 06:40, after the role sync and well clear of everything else. Nothing depends on it and it
# depends on nothing, so it goes where the box is quietest.
40 6 * * * root cd /srv/grims/repo && docker compose -f infra/docker/compose.prod.yml --env-file /srv/grims/.env --profile jobs run --rm worker node apps/worker/dist/reputation.js >> /var/log/grims-worker.log 2>&1

# Discord role reconciliation.
10 6 * * * root cd /srv/grims/repo && docker compose -f infra/docker/compose.prod.yml --env-file /srv/grims/.env --profile jobs run --rm worker node apps/worker/dist/role-sync.js >> /var/log/grims-worker.log 2>&1

# Elite Dangerous knowledge ingest. Coriolis changes only with a game update; Spansh rebuilds
# nightly. 03:20 UTC — off-peak, and BEFORE the 04:17 janitor so a long import is never competing
# with a prune for disk. The galaxy import also rebuilds market_entries when it finishes, so the
# route table can never disagree with the stations it was derived from.
20 3 * * * root cd /srv/grims/repo && docker compose -f infra/docker/compose.prod.yml --env-file /srv/grims/.env --profile jobs run --rm worker node apps/worker/dist/ingest-knowledge.js coriolis galaxy >> /var/log/grims-worker.log 2>&1

# Roster knowledge, from the Inara profile cache. ON ITS OWN — squadron owner, 2026-07-31: "the
# ingestion for ML from inara, that one must be batched and run on its own."
#
# 05:50, AFTER the 05:12 rank sweep that fills the cache it reads. Ordered rather than coincidental:
# running it first would ingest yesterday's roster every single day, and nothing would look wrong.
# Also clear of the 05:40 nickname audit — two `compose run` containers starting together on a
# one-vCPU box is how a job that normally takes seconds starts timing out.
#
# It makes no Inara requests of its own — see the job's own note on why that is the batching, not a
# shortcut around it.
50 5 * * * root cd /srv/grims/repo && docker compose -f infra/docker/compose.prod.yml --env-file /srv/grims/.env --profile jobs run --rm worker node apps/worker/dist/ingest-knowledge.js inara >> /var/log/grims-worker.log 2>&1
